Sunderland made their way to the final after starting in the Second Round, as they were a [[Premier League]] team not involved in either the [[UEFA Champions League]] or the [[UEFA Europa League]]. In that round, they defeated [[EFL League One|Football League One]] side [[Milton Keynes Dons F.C.|Milton Keynes Dons]] 4–2 at Sunderland's [[Stadium of Light]]. Trailing 2–0 with 15 minutes remaining, [[Jozy Altidore]], [[Connor Wickham]] (2) and [[Adam Johnson (footballer)|Adam Johnson]] scoring late goals to secure the win for Sunderland.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/23764127 |title=Sunderland 4–2 MK Dons |publisher=BBC Sport (British Broadcasting Corporation) |date=27 August 2013 |access-date=22 January 2014 }}</ref> In the Third Round, they drew League One side [[Peterborough United F.C.|Peterborough United]], whom they defeated 2–0 at the Stadium of Light. This game marked [[Kevin Ball]]'s first win while in temporary charge of the Black Cats, following [[Paolo Di Canio]]'s sacking.<ref>{{cite news |title=Sunderland 2–0 Peterborough |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/24122228 |publisher=BBC Sport (British Broadcasting Corporation) |date=24 September 2013 |access-date=22 January 2014 }}</ref> In the Fourth Round, Sunderland (now managed by [[Gus Poyet]]) defeated fellow Premier League team [[Southampton F.C.|Southampton]], again at the Stadium of Light, 2–1.<ref>{{cite web |first=Saj |last=Chowdhury |title=Sunderland 2–1 Southampton |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/24739094 |publisher=BBC Sport (British Broadcasting Corporation) |date=6 November 2013 |access-date=22 January 2014 }}</ref>

In the Fifth Round, they were drawn against fellow Premier League team [[Chelsea F.C.|Chelsea]], once again at the Stadium of Light. The Black Cats found themselves 1–0 down after a [[Frank Lampard]] shot deflected in off [[Lee Cattermole]] a minute after half time. However, former Chelsea striker [[Fabio Borini]] equalised in the 88th minute to take the game to [[Overtime (sports)#Association football|extra time]]. With penalties looming, [[Ki Sung-yueng]] scored two minutes from the final whistle to give Sunderland a 2–1 win and passage to the semi-finals.<ref>{{cite news |first=Aimee |last=Lewis |title=Sunderland 2–1 Chelsea |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/25312545 |publisher=BBC Sport (British Broadcasting Corporation) |date=17 December 2013 |access-date=22 January 2014 }}</ref>

In the semi-final, they were drawn with reigning Premier League champions [[Manchester United F.C.|Manchester United]]. In the first leg, the Black Cats battled to a 2–1 victory at the Stadium of Light; a [[Ryan Giggs]] own goal gave the home side the lead on the stroke of half time, [[Nemanja Vidić]] levelled for the visitors in the second half before [[Tom Cleverley]] fouled Adam Johnson in the box, and Borini converted the penalty.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Cryer |first1=Andy |title=Sunderland 2-1 Manchester United |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/25556245 |access-date=22 September 2020 |work=BBC Sport |date=7 January 2014}}</ref> Almost 9,000 Sunderland fans travelled to Manchester for the second leg on 22 January, with the club providing free coaches for their supporters.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Walker |first1=Michael |title=David Moyes is a proven manager who doesn't deserve the daily scrutiny and mockery |url=https://www.irishtimes.com/sport/soccer/english-soccer/david-moyes-is-a-proven-manager-who-doesn-t-deserve-the-daily-scrutiny-and-mockery-1.1667388 |access-date=22 September 2020 |work=The Irish Times |date=25 January 2014}}</ref> In the second leg at [[Old Trafford]], Sunderland secured their place in the final in dramatic fashion. United led 1–0 after 90 minutes thanks to a headed goal from former Sunderland loanee [[Jonny Evans]] to take the tie to extra time. Due to the away goals rule becoming active, Sunderland needed to score to avoid elimination. In the 119th minute, a shot from [[Phil Bardsley]] (six years to the day after he joined Sunderland from United) was pushed into his own goal by United's goalkeeper [[David de Gea]], sparking celebrations from the Sunderland players and fans. However, United immediately attacked down the other end, where [[Javier Hernández]] scored to make the score 2–1 on the night and 3–3 on aggregate, sending the game to a penalty shoot-out, which Sunderland won 2–1. [[Vito Mannone]] saved two penalties, including the decisive one from [[Rafael (footballer, born 1990)|Rafael]], to send the Black Cats to the new [[Wembley Stadium|Wembley]] for the first time.<ref>{{cite news |title=Sunderland beat Manchester United in dramatic shootout to reach final |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2014/jan/22/manchester-united-sunderland-capital-one-cup-match-report |work=The Guardian |publisher=Guardian News and Media |date=22 January 2014 |access-date=23 January 2014 }}</ref>

==Build-up==
[[File:Zabaleta Negredo league cup.JPG|thumb|Manchester City's [[Pablo Zabaleta]] and [[Álvaro Negredo]] holding the trophy after the final]]
Sunderland were appearing in their first League Cup final since [[1985 Football League Cup final|1985]], when they lost 1–0 to [[Norwich City F.C.|Norwich City]].<ref>{{cite news |first=Simon |last=Turnbull |title=Sunderland rue another year of dashed dreams |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football-sunderland-rue-another-year-of-dashed-dreams-1157578.html |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20220501/https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football-sunderland-rue-another-year-of-dashed-dreams-1157578.html |archive-date=1 May 2022 |url-access=subscription |url-status=live |work=The Independent |publisher=Independent Print |date=27 May 1998 |access-date=22 January 2014}}{{cbignore}}</ref>

Each club received an allocation of 31,580 tickets for the match, priced between £40 and £100.<ref>{{cite web |title=League Cup Final |url=http://www.wembleystadium.com/Events/2014/League-Cup-Final/2-March-2014 |publisher=The Football Association |access-date=26 January 2014 |archive-date=21 January 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140121191313/http://www.wembleystadium.com/Events/2014/League-Cup-Final/2-March-2014 |url-status=dead }}</ref>

==Match==
[[File:2014 Capital One Cup - before the game.jpg|thumb|Before kick-off]]

===Summary===
Sunderland took the lead in the 10th minute, when a ball over the top from [[Adam Johnson (footballer)|Adam Johnson]] found [[Fabio Borini]], who held off the challenge of [[Vincent Kompany]] before slotting the ball into the bottom right corner of [[Costel Pantilimon]]'s goal. Borini was played through again later in the first half, but Kompany made amends with a successful last-ditch sliding challenge. Sunderland held their lead until half time; however, they were undone by two City goals after the break. In the 55th minute, [[Yaya Touré]] curled the ball into the top-left corner of [[Vito Mannone]]'s goal from 25 yards out, and almost immediately from kick off City attacked again, with [[Samir Nasri]] firing home from the outside of his boot from a deflected [[Aleksandar Kolarov]] cross, leaving Mannone standing. Sunderland pressed for an equaliser, with substitute [[Steven Fletcher (footballer)|Steven Fletcher]] wasting a last-minute chance by failing to control the ball, before [[Jesús Navas]] finished off a swift City counter-attack in stoppage time to seal their first League Cup win since 1976.<ref>{{cite news |first=Phil |last=McNulty |title=Man City 3-1 Sunderland |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/26311649 |publisher=BBC Sport (British Broadcasting Corporation) |date=2 March 2014 |access-date=23 April 2015 }}</ref>

